{
"A syllogism is discourse in which, certain things being stated, something other than what is stated follows of necessity from their being so.": "10aca308d67a",
"A wet street does not rule out other causes.": "7ae377b92178",
"An argument that is valid and also has true premises is called sound. Soundness is what we actually want, because only a sound argument guarantees its conclusion. The division of labor is worth noticing. Whether an argument is valid can be settled from an armchair. Whether its premises are true usually cannot: that depends on biology, or history, or whatever else the premises bear on. Logic transmits truth from premises to conclusion. It does not supply the truth in the first place.": "75493937d3cb",
"An argument whose premises assume its conclusion begs the question.": "ecd32697625d",
"Aristotle (384–322 BCE) saw that this necessity has nothing to do with Socrates or with death. It holds by virtue of the pattern alone. Replace the terms with letters, as he was the first to do, and the pattern still compels: if A belongs to every B, and B belongs to every C, then A belongs to every C. Whatever you put in for the letters, true premises will never lead to a false conclusion. With that step, the study of reasoning became a science of forms.": "24dc3fed84f3",
"Buddhist philosophers had a logic of their own, hetuvidyā, the science of reasons, known in Chinese as 因明. Around 500 CE the logician Dignāga reduced the Nyāyas five steps to three. Thesis: sound is impermanent. Reason: because it is produced. Example: whatever is produced is impermanent, like a pot; and nothing permanent is produced, like space.": "ee889f026bca",
"Everything so far concerns deduction, where the premises are meant to guarantee the conclusion. Most reasoning in science and in life is of another kind. From every swan observed so far has been white to all swans are white is an inductive step: the conclusion says more than the premises do, and so it can be false even when they are true. Inductive arguments are therefore never valid, and it would be a mistake to blame them for that. They are judged by strength, and a strong one makes its conclusion probable. Whether that standard can itself be justified is a famous problem, taken up in lesson A8.": "d3da76754963",
"If A is predicated of all B, and B of all C, A must be predicated of all C.": "d3d0624cce6c",
"If you grant the two premises you cannot consistently deny the conclusion.": "e5246900d2c5",
"In ordinary speech an argument is a quarrel. In philosophy it is something quieter and more useful: a set of statements, the premises, offered as reasons for accepting another statement, the conclusion. To give an argument is to put your reasons on the table where other people can inspect them. There are then exactly two ways it can fail. One or more of the premises may be false, or the conclusion may not follow from the premises even if they are all true. The whole of logic grows out of keeping these two failures apart.": "0c4d3b0b455e",
"It holds by virtue of the pattern alone.": "652a9e8f65ed",
"It may seem that validity is trivially easy to get, and in a sense it is: for any conclusion at all, one can invent premises that entail it. That is why a valid argument settles nothing by itself, and why serious disagreement is nearly always about premises. Yet the discipline of form is what makes such disagreement productive. Set an opponents reasoning out in valid form, supplying in its strongest version whatever was left unsaid, and you will see which premise carries the weight. You then know precisely what the two of you disagree about, and in turn what evidence would bear on it. Philosophers call this the principle of charity. It is good manners, and it is also the fastest way to find out who is right.": "ef04d9c2e29c",
"Logic was invented at least three times. In China the later Mohists, in the third century BCE, analyzed disputation and held that an explanation must bring out the reason for a claim: 以說出故. In India the Nyāya school set out a five-step inference that became the common property of Hindu and Buddhist philosophers: the hill has fire; because it has smoke; wherever there is smoke there is fire, as in a kitchen; this hill has smoke; therefore it has fire.": "b22d554f41b6",
"Much everyday reasoning runs on if. Two patterns are valid. Modus ponens: if P then Q; P; therefore Q. Modus tollens: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P. Each has an invalid twin that looks almost the same. Affirming the consequent: if it rained, the street is wet; the street is wet; therefore it rained. (A street cleaner may have passed.) Denying the antecedent: if it rained, the street is wet; it did not rain; therefore the street is not wet. A wet street does not rule out other causes, and that is all it takes for both twins to fail.": "f9b2c3efd12e",
"Replace the terms with letters and the pattern still compels.": "2ba0bf470652",
"Set beside Aristotles syllogism, the form is close: the example states the general rule, the reason applies it, and the thesis follows. The difference is the pot. A Buddhist inference is incomplete until the rule has been tied to a case that both parties accept, and tested against a contrasting case. Mill complained that the syllogism never tells us how its general premise is known. The Indian logicians had built an answer to that complaint into the form itself, some thirteen centuries earlier. Whether an example can really support a universal rule is, of course, the problem of induction again (Dharma and the West, D14).": "3f4da5b89a66",
"Take the most famous example in the subject. All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal. If you grant the two premises you cannot consistently deny the conclusion. An argument with this property is called valid: it is impossible for all of its premises to be true while its conclusion is false. The premises are then said to entail the conclusion.": "835724d22bae",
"That depends on whatever the premises bear on.": "6dd66721bfd5",
"The Indian pattern differs from Aristotles in one instructive way. It insists on an example, the kitchen, so that the general rule is tied to a case both parties have seen. A Greek syllogism asks only whether the conclusion follows. An Indian inference asks in addition how the rule is known. That second question, as we shall see, is exactly where Aristotles critics attacked.": "217ebd904048",
"The definition has a consequence that surprises almost everyone at first. Validity says nothing about whether the premises are in fact true. All fish can fly; all whales are fish; therefore all whales can fly is a perfectly valid argument. Every statement in it is false, and yet the conclusion follows: if the premises were true, the conclusion would have to be.": "f2a77846e3ab",
"The necessity holds whatever the argument is about.": "3810ceed691b",
"The reverse also happens. All poets are human; Li Bai is human; therefore Li Bai is a poet has two true premises and a true conclusion, and it is invalid. The quickest way to see this is to keep the form and change the content: all poets are human; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is a poet. The premises are still true and the conclusion is now false, so the form cannot be trusted, and it never could. This is the method of counterexample, and it is the logicians basic instrument.": "787880250755",
"Validity may seem trivially easy to get.": "a7dfcd93db86",
"Whether an argument is valid can be settled from an armchair.": "ee535cd395b7",
"You know what you disagree about, and in turn what evidence would bear on it.": "fa50149f0cf7",
